# A run stopped while its command runs says so before it waits on anything, hands the command
# the signal, and ends as 128 plus the signal's number once the command is gone, whatever the
# command exits with (#24; today's devlab died without a word). Only devlab is signalled here, so
# what the command shows it got from devlab.
& r a@demo-a exec devlab run --no-device -- sh -c 'trap "kill \$!; echo got TERM; exit 0" TERM; echo started; sleep 30 & wait'
until r started
signal r TERM
wait r
1> started
1> got TERM
2> devlab: lease 1, slot 0 (stack http://127.0.0.1:27321, down)
2> devlab: sh stopped by SIGTERM after <n> s
? 143
& i a@demo-a exec devlab run --no-device --quiet -- sh -c 'trap "kill \$!; echo got INT; exit 3" INT; echo started; sleep 30 & wait'
until i started
signal i INT
wait i
1> started
1> got INT
2> devlab: sh stopped by SIGINT after <n> s
? 130
